steps.say:
A long if / else if chain would work here, but switch says it more plainly. This is the modern arrow form:
int coins = 2;
switch (coins) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("bread");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("cheese");
    default -> System.out.println("nothing");
}
Java takes the value of coins, finds the case that matches it, and runs only what follows that arrow. default is the door for “none of the above”.

beats.code:
int coins = 2;
switch (coins) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("bread");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("cheese");
    default -> System.out.println("nothing");
}

steps.say:
A switch can also BE a value. This is a switch expression: each arrow hands back a value, and the whole switch is stored in a variable.
String food = switch (coins) {
    case 1 -> "bread";
    case 2, 3 -> "cheese";
    default -> "nothing";
};
case 2, 3 means “2 or 3”. And see the semicolon after the closing brace? The whole thing is one assignment statement.

beats.code:
String food = switch (coins) {
    case 1 -> "bread";
    case 2, 3 -> "cheese";
    default -> "nothing";
};

steps.say:
In older code you will meet the colon form. Read it; do not copy it.
switch (coins) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("bread");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("cheese");
        break;
}
With colons, Java does not stop at the end of a case. It keeps running down into the next case (this is called fall-through) until it meets break. Forget one break and two cases run. Arrows have no fall-through, which is why we prefer them.

beats.code:
switch (coins) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("bread");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("cheese");
        break;
}

